WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:02.640
Have you ever wondered

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.560
if there are more natural ways
to lower your high blood pressure,

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.520
guard against Alzheimer's,
lose weight, and feel better?

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.720
Well, it turns out there is!

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.200
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.480
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.920
Did you know the secretion of melatonin
appears to progressively decline with age,

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.200
dropping as much as 70%
between middle age and older age?

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.120
Here’s our first story.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.520
Some experts recommend melatonin

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.320
as a first-line agent
to treat insomnia

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.920
in older adults.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.160
The World Sleep Society
disagrees

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.240
due to its low efficacy.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.800
Subjectively,
people report better sleep,

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.280
though objectively,
a meta-analysis of studies

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.200
found that melatonin
only helped people

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.600
get to sleep 4 minutes faster

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.440
and extend
overall sleep duration

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.440
by about 13 minutes.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.160
If you do want to try melatonin
to help with sleep,

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.360
older adults are advised

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.680
to use the smallest
effective dose

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.440
an hour before bedtime,

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.640
starting perhaps
at 0.3 milligrams.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.960
The 12-milligram supplements
on the market

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.400
could produce levels
in the bloodstream

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.040
that can be
two hundred times higher

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.720
than normal nightly levels,
which raises safety concerns.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.720
After all,
melatonin used to be known

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.280
as the anti-gonad hormone,

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.000
with human-equivalent doses
of just a milligram or two

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.840
reducing the size of sex organs

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.800
and impairing fertility
in laboratory animals.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.120
Furthermore,
due to poor regulation

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.160
of dietary supplements,
in the U.S.,

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.400
you never know
what you're gonna be getting.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.480
An analysis
of thirty-one different brands

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.000
of melatonin supplements

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.640
found that the actual
melatonin content varied

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.800
up to nearly 500 percent

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.440
compared to what was listed
on the bottle.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.600
Even within a single brand,

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.160
different lots
of the same product

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.800
suffered similar variability.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.400
Given that there is
no guarantee

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.720
of the strength or purity
of melatonin supplements,

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.640
many countries have banned
over-the-counter melatonin,

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.240
making it prescription-only

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.520
so authenticity
could be ensured.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.640
U.S. citizens can get
certified melatonin

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.760
by sending a prescription
to an online Canadian pharmacy

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.760
(though it's
technically illegal)

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.360
or can get melatonin
from their diet.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.240
Interestingly, the rise
in melatonin blood levels

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.680
or metabolites in the urine

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.080
consistently exceeds
the amount of melatonin

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.960
that's actually ingested
in these foods.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.720
You can even get a spike
in melatonin

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.200
after eating fruit
that doesn't even appear

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.320
to have any detectable
melatonin at all.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.440
The answer to the puzzle
may lie in the gut.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.240
The first site
outside the pineal gland

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.800
where melatonin production
was discovered

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.200
was the human appendix.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.440
Although we now know
melatonin can be produced

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.560
throughout the body,

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.400
the greatest accumulation
appears to be

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.560
in the gastrointestinal tract,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.240
estimated to contain more than
400 times more melatonin

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.160
than the pineal gland.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.560
So, perhaps the consumption
of certain foods

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.800
can cause the release
of some of this bounty

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.480
into the bloodstream.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.800
This may be
how magnesium supplements

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.720
(500 milligrams a day
for eight weeks)

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:11.280
was able to improve
sleep quality and quantity

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.280
over placebo, given the effect
was accompanied by a rise

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.200
in melatonin levels.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.360
More intriguing are its
purported anti-aging benefits.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.280
In addition to regulating sleep

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.560
and our circadian rhythms
(when properly timed

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.040
it can be used
to treat jet lag),

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.880
melatonin can have antioxidant

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.120
and anti-inflammatory
properties.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.200
However, secretion of melatonin

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.480
appears to progressively
decline with age,

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.320
dropping as much as 70 percent
between middle and older age.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:43.920
This has led some to speculate

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.000
that "aging is a syndrome of
relative melatonin deficiency

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.760
resulting from
the gradual failure

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.600
of the pineal gland."

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.240
A variety
of age-related diseases

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.160
such as Alzheimer's
are associated

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.720
with particularly low levels
of melatonin,

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.320
but we have yet to determine

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.760
if this is a cause
or consequence of disease.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.400
Melatonin supplementation
extends the lifespan

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.800
of fruit flies but shortens
the lifespan of C. elegans.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.200
Mice that have had
their pineal glands removed

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.760
appeared to age more rapidly,
but when put to the test,

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:21.520
melatonin prolonged survival
of some strains of mice,

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:24.960
but shortened the lives
of others (by inducing cancer).

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.840
So, are we more like
New Zealand black mice

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.720
or Bagg albino mice?

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.560
Sometimes
the same research group

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.960
went on to publish
opposite results,

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.640
showing melatonin
both increasing

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:38.720
and decreasing lifespan.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.800
Not surprisingly,
a meta-analysis found

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:41.760
no overall effect

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.480
when all of the mouse studies
were considered together.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:48.720
In rats, melatonin
significantly improved survival,

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.040
but so did
a melatonin-blocking drug.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:58.109
In our next story, we ask
is six hours of sleep enough?

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.360
Conventional wisdom has it
that over the last 50 years

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:05.120
sleep duration has declined
in parallel with the increasing

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.920
prevalence of obesity,
suggesting that an epidemic

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:12.160
of sleep loss is associated with
the epidemic of weight gain.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.920
Now, we have triple digit streaming
TV channels, smartphones,

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:18.800
and tablets to keep us entertained
well into the night.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.000
"The hurry and excitement
of modern life is quite correctly

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.520
held to be responsible
for much of the insomnia,"

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.320
concluded one medical
journal editorial.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:33.120
But that was an editorial
published in 1894.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.320
Are we really sleeping
that much less?

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:39.520
Since 1905, sleep duration
in children and adolescents

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:42.320
has declined by a little
over an hour a night.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.640
However, child labor wasn't
outlawed until 1938 though,

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:49.520
and so part of the explanation
may be due to the exhaustion

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.480
of sweating it out in the mines,
farms, and factories

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:54.640
in the early part of the last century.

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:57.840
Since 1970, youth sleep duration
has only declined about

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:58.960
15 minutes per night,

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:03.600
and it's not clear if sleep duration
in adults has changed much at all.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:07.840
Based on 168 studies of objective
measurements of sleep duration,

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.520
instead of just self-report,

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.560
sleep duration in adults --
objective total sleep time --

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.320
has't changed much since 1960.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.040
Since 2003, average sleep
duration in the United States

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.120
may have even gone up.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:22.880
Now, of course, just because
we don't have evidence

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:26.560
that there's been a growing
epidemic of sleep deprivation

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.440
that doesn't necessarily mean
we're getting enough sleep.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:32.720
Maybe we weren't getting enough
sleep 50 years ago either,

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:34.800
or since Edison's lightbulbs,

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:38.960
or since candles were
invented 5,000 years ago.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:42.240
How might we determine
the optimal sleep duration?

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:44.800
One way would be to study
millions of people and see

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.120
how many hours a night is
associated with the longest lifespan.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.440
Sleep is a great mystery.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:55.760
A trait shared across animal species,
sleep must be of vital importance

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:00.720
to survive natural selection pressures
to eliminate such a vulnerable state.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.600
Indeed, cringe-worthy
experiments have shown that

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.960
keeping animals awake long enough
is fatal within 11 to 32 days.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:12.400
One of the functions of sleep that
has been elucidated in recent years

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.040
is the clearance of toxic
waste substances

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:16.800
that build up during the day

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.320
through a newly discovered
drainage system in the brain.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:22.480
This could help explain why
those who routinely get

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:25.840
less than seven hours of sleep
at night may be at increased risk

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:29.280
of developing cognitive
disorders such as dementia.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.960
Even a single all-nighter can
cause a significant increase

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:36.480
in beta amyloid accumulation
in critical brain areas,

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:41.520
a gummy substance implicated in the
development of Alzheimer's disease.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:44.400
The lowest risk for developing
cognitive impairment was found

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.200
for those getting seven to
eight hours of sleep a night,

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.560
which is the same optimal range
found for diabetes risk,

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:54.000
based on 36 studies following
more than a million people.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:57.120
The increased risk associated
with getting only six hours a night

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.040
compared to seven or eight
is comparable

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.760
to the increase in diabetes risk
linked to physical inactivity.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:07.840
For death from all causes combined,
there's been more than 50 studies

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:11.520
following hundreds of thousands
of people for up to 34 years.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:14.720
Sleeping too short and too long
are both associated

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:16.160
with cutting one's life short,

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.920
with the apparent sweet spot
at seven hours a night.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:22.720
Seven hours may seem short,
but that may actually be

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:24.720
what's natural for our species.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.000
Scientists studied three isolated
pre-industrial societies

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.280
across two continents and
found a surprising uniformity.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:33.760
Despite no electric lighting
or gadgets,

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:35.600
they stayed up until
about three hours

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:38.800
after sunset and then
typically rose before dawn,

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:41.600
accumulating about a solid
six and a half hours of sleep

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:45.520
out of about seven and
a half hours in "bed."

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.600
A mechanism by which excess sleep
might be harmful remains elusive,

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:52.480
and so the association between
increased risk of death and disease

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:56.320
and sleeping nine or more hours
a night has been largely dismissed

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:58.240
as implausible.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.840
Maybe its reverse causation,

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:03.200
sickness leading to more time
in bed instead of vice-versa,

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:04.480
or confounding factors

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:06.240
such as employment status.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:08.320
After all, who gets to sleep in?

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.560
Those without a job to get to.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:13.520
However, there is experimental
evidence showing negative

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:15.680
health effects from insufficient sleep.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:16.960
So in terms of sleeping

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:22.880
in the sweet spot, aim for at least
seven hours of regular sleep a day.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:23.680
Finally today,

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:29.200
we look at how one function of sleep may
be the clearance of toxic waste byproducts

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:34.080
through a newly discovered
drainage system in the brain.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:37.920
Sleep is a great mystery.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:39.840
A trait shared
across animal species,

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:41.840
sleep must be
of vital importance

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.840
to survive
natural selection pressures

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:46.880
to eliminate
such a vulnerable state.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:50.480
Indeed, cringeworthy experiments
have shown

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:52.320
that keeping animals awake
long enough

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.320
can be fatal within
eleven to thirty-two days.

00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:59.520
It turns out
sleep is of-the-brain,

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:02.480
by the brain,
and for the brain.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.680
One function of sleep that has
been elucidated in recent years

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:09.040
is the clearance
of toxic waste byproducts

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:12.640
through a newly discovered
drainage system in the brain.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:15.280
With the invention
of the encephalogram, EEG,

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:17.200
to measure brain wave activity,

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:19.520
the scientific world
was quickly disabused

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:22.800
of the notion that sleep was
a time of rest for the brain.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.480
During certain stages of sleep

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:27.360
there is brain-wide activity
going on,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:30.560
but what was
the brain actively doing?

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:32.640
More than 2,000 years ago,

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:36.800
Aristotle proposed that sleep
helps the body clean the blood.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:41.360
Today, we know sleep may help
the body clean the brain.

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:45.360
Until 2012, we thought
that the brain was singular

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:49.200
among organs for recycling
nearly all of its own waste.

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:50.880
It had to,
since it was separated

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:54.560
from the rest of the body
by the blood-brain barrier.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:57.600
But the barrier that keeps
toxins out of the brain

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:00.880
presumably keeps toxins in.

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:02.480
Then, in 2012,

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.520
a brain-wide
fluid transport network

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:09.920
was discovered,
termed the glymphatic system.

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:12.480
By microscopically tracking dye

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:13.920
injected into the brains
of mice,

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.720
scientists discovered
fluid-filled tunnels

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:19.040
surrounding blood vessels
in the brain.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:21.200
The pressure wave
of arterial pulses

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.920
with every heartbeat
milks the fluid along

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:26.720
before eventually draining
into the cerebrospinal fluid

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:28.960
surrounding the brain.

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:31.440
What does this
have to do with sleep?

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:35.360
The whole system is only
really active when sleeping.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:38.160
During wakefulness,
these tunnels are clamped down,

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:41.520
reducing glymphatic flow
by 90 percent.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:44.080
The thought is the fluid shifts
might interfere

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:47.040
with targeted neurotransmitter
chemical communication

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:48.640
in the awake state.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:52.480
So the biological need for sleep
may reflect the need

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:55.200
for the brain to enter
into a state to filter out

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:58.400
potentially neurotoxic waste
products like beta amyloid,

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:01.520
which is implicated
in Alzheimer's disease.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:03.360
Perhaps this could help explain

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:06.160
why those who routinely get
fewer than seven hours of sleep

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:07.600
a night are at increased risk

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:11.360
of developing cognitive
disorders such as dementia.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:14.480
Randomizing individuals
to have their sleep disrupted

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:15.840
by a series of beeps

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:18.640
administered through headphones
in a sleep lab

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:22.400
increases amyloid levels,
whereas improving sleep,

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:26.240
by treating sleep apnea patients
with CPAP, for example,

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:28.160
improves slow wave activity,

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:31.760
deep sleep, and appears
to lower amyloid levels.

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:34.560
PET scans show
even a single all-nighter

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:36.080
can cause
a significant increase

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:40.400
in accumulation of beta amyloid
in critical brain areas.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:44.240
The problem is that
glymphatic brain filtration

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:47.040
appears to decline with aging.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:49.840
Old mice only have
10 to 20 percent

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:52.480
the glymphatic function
of young mice.

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:54.320
This could be due
to a number of factors.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:55.280
As we age,

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:58.400
we experience less
of the deep, slow wave sleep,

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:00.880
the type of sleep during
which brain waste clearance

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:03.360
appears to be the most active.

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:06.000
Further contributing
to the stagnancy,

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:08.720
our arteries tend
to stiffen as we age,

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:12.960
reducing the pulsations
that drive the glymphatic pump.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:15.120
That also offers
one potential explanation

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:17.920
as to why hypertension
is tied to dementia.

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:20.560
The thickening of artery walls
with high blood pressure

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:23.039
also has a stiffening effect.

